@noindent
The environment is crucial to the evaluation process, because it determines the
context in which an expression should be evaluated.  Indeed, one could say that
expressions in a programming language do not, in themselves, have any meaning.
Rather, an expression acquires a meaning only with respect to some environment
in which it is evaluated.  Even the interpretation of an expression as
straightforward as @code{1 + 1} depends on an understanding that one is
operating in a context in which @code{+} is the symbol for addition.  Thus, in
our model of evaluation we will always speak of evaluating an expression with
respect to some environment.  To describe programs, we will suppose that there
is a global environment, consisting of a single frame (with no enclosing
environment) that holds the program's top-level definitions.  In Rust this
frame is the crate's collection of items, and the names in it are resolved
when the program is compiled.  The frames that exist at run time, and the ones
this chapter is about, are the ones that hold a function's parameters, local
bindings, and captured variables.  For example, the idea that @code{+} is the
symbol for addition is captured by saying that the operator belongs to the
language itself, outside every frame; what an environment supplies is the
meaning of each name that a function body merely uses.

