@node	3.2.1, 3.2.2, 3.2, 3.2
@subsection The Rules for Evaluation

The overall specification of how a call is evaluated
remains the same as when we first introduced it in @ref{1.1.3}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
To evaluate a function call:

@end itemize

@enumerate 1

@item
Evaluate the subexpressions of the call.@footnote{Assignment introduces a
subtlety into step 1 of the evaluation rule.  As shown in @ref{Exercise 3.8},
the presence of assignment allows us to write expressions that will produce
different values depending on the order in which the subexpressions in a
call are evaluated.  Rust leaves the order in which a call's arguments are
evaluated unspecified, for exactly the book's reason: the order is an
implementation detail, and one should never write programs that depend on
some particular order.  A sophisticated compiler might optimize a program by
varying the order in which subexpressions are evaluated.}

@item
Apply the value of the operator subexpression to the values of the operand
subexpressions.

@end enumerate

@noindent
The environment model of evaluation replaces the substitution model in
specifying what it means to apply a function to arguments.

In the environment model of evaluation, a procedure is always a pair consisting
of some code and a pointer to an environment.  Procedures are created in one
way only: by evaluating a function value.  In Rust the function values are
@newterm{closures}.  The compiler gathers each variable that a closure body
uses into a compiler-generated struct built for that one closure, and the
struct is the procedure object: its fields are the environment part, and the
call body the compiler generates for it is the code.  A @code{move} closure
takes ownership of each binding it names.  When a binding lives behind an
@code{Rc}, what moves is a clone of the pointer rather than the binding, so
the closure and the rest of the program keep pointing at one shared frame;
@code{Rc::clone} is the book's ``pointer to an environment'' made explicit in
syntax.  For example, consider the function definition

@example rust
fn square(x: i128) -> i128 @{
    x * x
@}
@end example

@noindent
written in the global environment of a crate.  The @code{fn} syntax is the
everyday spelling of a function.  It would have been equivalent to have used
the closure form that builds a function value and binds a name to it,

@example rust
let square = |x: i128| x * x;
@end example

@noindent
which evaluates the closure expression @code{|x: i128| x * x} and binds
@code{square} to the resulting value, all in the environment that runs the
binding.  The two forms differ in one respect that the model makes visible: a
nested @code{fn} item is compiled like a top-level function and captures
nothing, so its environment part is always the crate's global environment,
while a closure carries whatever frame it was built in.

@ref{Figure 3.2} shows the result of evaluating this @code{let} expression.
The procedure object is a pair whose code specifies that the procedure has one
formal parameter, namely @code{x}, and a procedure body @code{x * x}.  The
environment part of the procedure is a pointer to the global environment, since
that is the environment in which the closure expression was evaluated to
produce the procedure. A new binding, which associates the procedure object
with the symbol @code{square}, has been added to the global frame.  In general,
a @code{let} creates a binding in the frame that runs it, and the top-level
items of a crate fill its global frame when the program is compiled.

