@noindent
The environment model of procedure application can be summarized by two rules:

@itemize @bullet

@item
A procedure object is applied to a set of arguments by constructing a frame,
binding the formal parameters of the procedure to the arguments of the call,
and then evaluating the body of the procedure in the context of the new
environment constructed.  The new frame has as its enclosing environment the
environment part of the procedure object being applied.  In Rust this frame is
the call's stack frame, and the environment part is the closure's captured
variables, or nothing at all for a plain @code{fn}.

@item
A procedure is created by evaluating a closure expression relative to a
given environment.  The resulting procedure object is a pair consisting of the
body of the closure expression and the captured bindings, which are the
pointer to the environment in which the procedure was created.

@end itemize

@noindent
We also specify that binding a name with @code{let} creates a binding in
the current environment frame and gives it the indicated
value.@footnote{If there is already a binding for the variable in the current
frame, Rust resolves the matter differently from the book's @code{define}: a
second @code{let} with the same name shadows the first, and changing
the value of an existing binding requires declaring it with @code{mut}.  This
keeps the issues of assignment visible in the source instead of letting
redefinition change values silently.} Finally, we
specify the behavior of assignment, the operation that forced us to introduce
the environment model in the first place.  Assigning to
@code{⟨@var{variable}⟩} in some environment locates
the binding of the variable in the environment and changes that binding to indicate
the new value.  That is, one finds the first frame in the environment that
contains a binding for the variable and modifies that frame; for a captured
binding, that frame is the closure object holding it, so assignment through
the closure reaches exactly the state the closure owns.  If the variable
is unbound in the environment, the program does not compile: the model's error
for assignment to an unbound variable is, in Rust, a compile-time error, and
assignment never creates a binding.

These evaluation rules, though considerably more complex than the substitution
model, are still reasonably straightforward.  Moreover, the evaluation model,
though abstract, provides a correct description of how a Rust program
evaluates a call.  In @ref{Chapter 4} we shall see how this model can
serve as a blueprint for implementing a working interpreter.  The following
sections elaborate the details of the model by analyzing some illustrative
programs.

@node	3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.2.1, 3.2
@subsection Applying Simple Procedures

When we introduced the substitution model in @ref{1.1.5} we showed how
the call @code{f(5)} evaluates to 136, given the following function
definitions:

@example rust
fn square(x: i128) -> i128 @{
    x * x
@}

fn sum_of_squares(x: i128, y: i128) -> i128 @{
    square(x) + square(y)
@}

fn f(a: i128) -> i128 @{
    sum_of_squares(a + 1, a * 2)
@}
@end example

@noindent
We can analyze the same example using the environment model.  @ref{Figure 3.4}
shows the three procedure objects created by evaluating the definitions of
@code{f}, @code{square}, and @code{sum_of_squares} in the global environment.
Each procedure object consists of some code, together with a pointer to the
global environment.  These three are plain @code{fn} items, so the pointer is
all there is: none of them captures anything.

