@noindent
To evaluate this call, we first evaluate the subexpressions.  The operator,
@code{sum_of_squares}, has a value that is a procedure object.
(Notice how this value is found: We first look in the first frame of E1, which
contains no binding for @code{sum_of_squares}.  Then we proceed to the
enclosing environment, i.e. the global environment, and find the binding shown
in @ref{Figure 3.4}; in a compiled Rust program this lookup has already
happened, once and for all, when the program was built.)  The other two
subexpressions are evaluated by applying
the primitive operations @code{+} and @code{*} to the two operand
expressions @code{a + 1} and @code{a * 2} to obtain 6 and 10, respectively.

@example rust
f(5)
// => 136
@end example

@noindent
Now we apply the procedure object @code{sum_of_squares} to the arguments 6 and
10.  This results in a new environment E2 in which the formal parameters
@code{x} and @code{y} are bound to the arguments.  Within E2 we evaluate the
body @code{square(x) + square(y)}.  This leads us to evaluate
@code{square(x)}, where @code{square} is found in the global frame and
@code{x} is 6.  Once again, we set up a new environment, E3, in which @code{x}
is bound to 6, and within this we evaluate the body of @code{square}, which is
@code{x * x}.  Also as part of applying @code{sum_of_squares}, we must
evaluate the subexpression @code{square(y)}, where @code{y} is 10.  This
second call to @code{square} creates another environment, E4, in which
@code{x}, the formal parameter of @code{square}, is bound to 10.  And within E4
we must evaluate @code{x * x}.

The important point to observe is that each call to @code{square} creates a new
environment containing a binding for @code{x}.  We can see here how the
different frames serve to keep separate the different local variables all named
@code{x}.  Notice that each frame created by @code{square} points to the global
environment, since this is the environment indicated by the @code{square}
procedure object.

After the subexpressions are evaluated, the results are returned.  The values
generated by the two calls to @code{square} are added by @code{sum-of-squares},
and this result is returned by @code{f}.  Since our focus here is on the
environment structures, we will not dwell on how these returned values are
passed from call to call; however, this is also an important aspect of the
evaluation process, and we will return to it in detail in @ref{Chapter 5}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.9}Exercise 3.9:} @emph{SICP exercise 3.9 is about
Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the
number and teaches the same section material.}  In @ref{1.2.1} we analyzed
two functions for computing factorials, a
recursive version

@example rust
fn factorial(n: u64) -> u128 @{
    if n <= 1 @{
        1
    @} else @{
        u128::from(n) * factorial(n - 1)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
and an iterative version

@example rust
fn factorial_loop(n: u64) -> u128 @{
    let mut product = 1;
    for counter in 1..=n @{
        product *= u128::from(counter);
    @}
    product
@}
@end example

@noindent
Draw the stack of frames, in the style of @ref{Figure 3.5}, that exists at the
deepest point of the call @code{factorial(6)}: one stack frame per call, each
holding its own @code{n}, each returning into the frame that called it.  Then
instrument both versions with a depth counter that records the greatest depth
reached, and check your drawing: the recursive version should reach a depth of
6, one frame per call from 6 down to 1, while the loop version should never
leave depth 1.@footnote{The environment model will not clarify our claim in
@ref{1.2.1} that a procedure such as @code{fact-iter} can run in a constant
amount of space.  Rust makes no tail-call promise, so the loop is the only one
of the two versions that is guaranteed to run in constant space; a recursive
helper in tail position gives no such guarantee, and deep recursion on the
default thread can exhaust the stack.  We will discuss how an interpreter can
restore the guarantee in @ref{5.4}.}
@end quotation

@node	3.2.3, 3.2.4, 3.2.2, 3.2
@subsection Frames as the Repository of Local State

We can turn to the environment model to see how procedures and assignment can
be used to represent objects with local state.  As an example, consider the
``withdrawal processor'' from @ref{3.1.1} created by calling the
function

@example rust
fn make_withdraw(balance: i128) -> impl FnMut(i128) -> Reply @{
    move |amount| @{
        if balance >= amount @{
            balance -= amount;
            Reply::Balance(balance)
        @} else @{
            Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Let us describe the evaluation of

@example rust
let mut w1 = make_withdraw(100);
@end example

@noindent
followed by

@example rust
w1(50)
// => 50
@end example

@noindent
@ref{Figure 3.6} shows the result of defining the @code{make_withdraw}
function in the global environment.  This produces a procedure object that
contains a pointer to the global environment.  So far, this is no different
from the examples we have already seen, except that the body of the function
builds and returns a closure, and the closure names a variable, @code{balance},
that belongs to the call that built it.  The @code{move} in front of the
closure is what the model calls the environment part: the closure takes
ownership of @code{balance} instead of borrowing it, because the returned
object must outlive the call that created it.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.6}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{make-withdraw} in the global environment.

@example
          +---------------------------+
global -->| make-withdraw: --+        |
env       +------------------|--------+
                             |      ^
                             V      |
                         .---.---.  |
                         | O | O-+--+
                         `-|-^---'
                           |
                           V
         parameters: balance
         body: (lambda (amount)
                 (if (>= balance amount)
                     (begin (set! balance
                                  (- balance amount))
                            balance)
                     "Insufficient funds"))
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.6c,,128mm,Figure 3.6: Result of defining make-withdraw in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
