@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{make-withdraw} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
The interesting part of the computation happens when we apply the function
@code{make_withdraw} to an argument:

@example rust
let mut w1 = make_withdraw(100);
@end example

@noindent
We begin, as usual, by setting up an environment E1 in which the formal
parameter @code{balance} is bound to the argument 100.  Within this
environment, we evaluate the body of @code{make_withdraw}, namely the
closure expression.  This constructs a new procedure object, whose code
is the generated body of the closure and whose environment is E1, the
environment in which the closure was evaluated to produce the procedure.  In
Rust, E1 is a concrete thing: it is the closure object itself, a
compiler-generated struct with one field, the moved-in @code{balance}.  The
resulting procedure object is the value returned by the call to
@code{make_withdraw}.  The @code{let} binds it to the name @code{w1} in the
frame that runs the binding, here the top-level frame of the @code{main}
function.
@ref{Figure 3.7} shows the resulting environment structure.

