@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.10}Exercise 3.10:} @emph{SICP exercise 3.10 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the
number and teaches the same section material.}  In the @code{make_withdraw}
function, the local variable @code{balance} is created as a parameter of
@code{make_withdraw}.  We could also create the local state variable
explicitly, with an inner binding, as follows:

@example rust
fn make_withdraw_let(initial_amount: i128) -> impl FnMut(i128) -> Reply @{
    let mut balance = initial_amount;
    move |amount| @{
        if balance >= amount @{
            balance -= amount;
            Reply::Balance(balance)
        @} else @{
            Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

Recall from @ref{1.3.2} the book's observation that its @code{let} is simply
syntactic sugar for a procedure call; in the book's language the inner
@code{let} builds a frame of its own, so the
procedure object's environment pointer goes to the @code{let}'s frame and only
from there to the factory call's frame.  Rust has no such rule: a @code{let}
in the factory body is another name in the factory's own frame, and the
closure captures the variable it actually names.  Use the environment model to
analyze this alternate version of
@code{make_withdraw_let}, drawing figures like the ones above to illustrate the
interactions

@example rust
let mut w1 = make_withdraw_let(100);

w1(50)
// => 50

let mut w2 = make_withdraw_let(100);
@end example

Show that the two versions of @code{make_withdraw} create objects with the same
behavior.  Then trace where @code{balance} lives in each version: which
variables each closure captures, and what @code{std::mem::size_of_val}
reports for a closure of each version.  How do the structures differ for the
two versions, and what does the answer say about whether the extra
@code{let} built an extra frame?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.10a}Exercise 3.10a:} @emph{Exercise 3.10a is added
by this edition and extends exercise 3.10; SICP numbers stop at 3.10.}  The
@code{move} closure of exercise 3.10 took ownership of a plain integer, so the
capture copied the binding itself.  State often lives behind an @code{Rc}
instead, and then the capture copies the pointer, not the binding.  Consider
the shared-cell withdrawal processor:

@example rust
fn make_withdraw_cell(balance: Rc<Cell<i128>>) -> impl FnMut(i128) -> Reply @{
    move |amount| @{
        if balance.get() >= amount @{
            balance.set(balance.get() - amount);
            Reply::Balance(balance.get())
        @} else @{
            Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

Build one cell holding 100 and two withdrawal processors over clones of it,
then predict each answer before running:

@example rust
let cell = Rc::new(Cell::new(100));
let mut w1 = make_withdraw_cell(Rc::clone(&cell));
let mut w2 = make_withdraw_cell(Rc::clone(&cell));

w1(30)
// => 70

w2(20)
// => 50
@end example

Explain each answer: which of ``the pointer'' and ``the binding'' did each
@code{Rc::clone} copy, and why the two objects behave as one account here
while the two objects of exercise 3.10 behave as two.  Then compare
@code{std::mem::size_of_val} on a closure of exercise 3.10 with one of this
exercise, and state what the difference measures.  Finally, an outside
@code{Rc} handle to the cell can watch the balance change across calls of
@code{w1}; explain why no outside code can do the same for the closures of
exercise 3.10, and which of the two shapes the book's figure E1 describes.
@end quotation

@node	3.2.4, 3.3, 3.1, 3.2
@subsection Internal Definitions

Section @ref{1.1.8} introduced the idea that procedures can have internal
definitions, thus leading to a block structure as in the following function to
compute square roots:

@example rust
fn sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 @{
    // The internal definitions, as closures that see `x`.
    let good_enough = |guess: f64| (guess * guess - x).abs() < 0.001;
    let improve = |guess: f64| f64::midpoint(guess, x / guess);
    // The book's `sqrt-iter` recurses; a loop expresses the same
    // iteration, because Rust has no tail-call guarantee (section 1.2.1).
    let mut guess = 1.0;
    while !good_enough(guess) @{
        guess = improve(guess);
    @}
    guess
@}
@end example

@noindent
Now we can use the environment model to see why these internal definitions
behave as desired.  One Rust fact makes the model sharper here: a nested
@code{fn} item is compiled like a top-level function and cannot see the
enclosing frame at all, so the internal definitions that need @code{x} must be
closures.  Whether a procedure object has an environment part is a property
the compiler checks.
@ref{Figure 3.11} shows the point in the evaluation of the
expression @code{sqrt(2)} where the internal procedure @code{good_enough} has
been called for the first time with @code{guess} equal to 1.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.11}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.11:} @code{Sqrt} procedure with internal definitions.

@example
          +--------------------------------------------------+
global -->| sqrt: --+                                        |
env       |         |                                        |
          +---------|----------------------------------------+
                    V       ^                   ^
                .---.---.   |                   |
     +----------+-O | O-+---+        +----------+------------+
     |          `---^---'            | x: 2                  |
     V                         E1 -->| good-enough?: -+      |
parameters: x                        | improve: ...   |      |
body: (define good-enough? ...)      | sqrt-iter: ... |      |
      (define improve ...)           +----------------|------+
      (define sqrt-iter ...)          ^  ^            |     ^
      (sqrt-iter 1.0)                 |  |            V     |
                            +---------++ |        .---.---. |
                      E2 -->| guess: 1 | |        | O | O-+-+
                            +----------+ |        `-|-^---'
                      call to sqrt-iter  |          |
                                         |          V
                               +---------++    parameters: guess
                         E3 -->| guess: 1 |    body: (< (abs ...)
                               +----------+             ...)
                         call to good-enough?
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.11b,,147mm,Figure 3.11: Sqrt procedure with internal definitions.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.11b.std,,147mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.11:} @code{Sqrt} procedure with internal definitions.}
@end float

Observe the structure of the environment.  @code{Sqrt} is a name in the
global environment that is bound to a procedure object whose associated
environment is the global environment.  When @code{sqrt} was called, a new
environment E1 was formed, subordinate to the global environment, in which the
parameter @code{x} is bound to 2.  The body of @code{sqrt} was then evaluated
in E1.  Since the first expression in the body of @code{sqrt} is

@example rust
let good_enough = |guess: f64| (guess * guess - x).abs() < 0.001;
@end example

@noindent
evaluating this expression created the procedure @code{good_enough} as a value
in the environment E1.  To be more precise, the name @code{good_enough} was added
to the first frame of E1, bound to a procedure object whose associated
environment is E1: the closure carries a borrow of @code{x} out of that frame,
and the compiler checks that the borrow cannot outlive the call.  Similarly, @code{improve} was created
as a procedure in E1.  For conciseness, @ref{Figure 3.11} shows only the
procedure object for @code{good_enough}.  The book's third internal procedure,
@code{sqrt-iter}, recursed on itself; the loop keeps the same iteration with
one frame slot for @code{guess} reused on every pass, because no tail call is
guaranteed to reuse E1.

@noindent
After the local procedures were created, the iteration began with
@code{guess} bound to 1, still in environment E1.  So the procedure object
bound to @code{good_enough} in E1 was called with 1 as an argument.  This created an
environment E2 in which @code{guess}, the parameter of @code{good_enough}, is
bound to 1, and the call's stack frame holds it.  Each pass of the loop calls
@code{improve} the same way, in the environment the figure labels E3.  Although @code{sqrt}
and
@code{good_enough} both have a variable named @code{guess}, these are two
distinct local variables located in different frames.  Also, E2 and E3 both
have E1 as their enclosing environment, because the @code{good_enough} and
@code{improve} closures both carry their environment part out of E1.  One
consequence of this is that the symbol @code{x} that appears in the body of
@code{good_enough} will reference the binding of @code{x} that appears in E1,
namely the value of @code{x} with which the original @code{sqrt} procedure was
called; in Rust this is the borrow the compiler tracks.

The environment model thus explains the two key properties that make local
procedure definitions a useful technique for modularizing programs:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The names of the local procedures do not interfere with names external to the
enclosing function, because the local procedure names will be bound in the
frame that the function creates when it is run, rather than being bound in the
global environment.

@item
The local procedures can access the arguments of the enclosing function,
simply by using parameter names as free variables.  This is because the body of
the local procedure is evaluated in an environment that is subordinate to the
evaluation environment for the enclosing function.

@end itemize

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.11}Exercise 3.11:} @emph{SICP exercise 3.11 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the
number and teaches the same section material.}  In @ref{3.2.3} we saw how
the environment model described the behavior of procedures with local state.
Now we have seen how internal definitions work.  A typical message-passing
object contains both of these aspects.  Consider the bank account of
@ref{3.1.1}, whose @code{send} dispatches on a request:

@example rust
let acc = make_account(50);

acc.send(Request::Deposit(40))
// => 90

acc.send(Request::Withdraw(60))
// => 30

let acc2 = make_account(100);
@end example

Draw the environment structure this sequence generates: one account value per
factory call, the state cell each one owns, and the compiled body of
@code{send} that every account shares.  Then answer the book's three
questions with experiments instead of the drawing alone.  @code{Account}
already carries an identity test from @ref{3.1.1}, @code{same_object_as},
which compares the state cells of two names with @code{Rc::ptr_eq}; use it
to check a @code{clone} of @code{acc} against @code{acc}, and @code{acc}
against @code{acc2}.  Deposit through the second name
and read the balance through the first.  Where is the local state for
@code{acc} kept, how are the local states for the two accounts kept distinct,
and which parts of the structure are shared between @code{acc} and
@code{acc2}?  Which of the two kinds of sharing can an experiment in Rust
observe, and which one is settled by the compiler?
@end quotation

